Here is a minimal standalone repro, plus the root cause down to the exact line, and a proposed 4-line fix.

## Repro (3 files, no Fiddle needed — plain `electron` npm package)

**`main.js`**
```js
const { app, session } = require("electron");
const path = require("path");

app.commandLine.appendSwitch("js-flags", "--expose-gc");

app.whenReady().then(async () => {
  const ses = session.defaultSession;

  // The service-worker preload script is the crash precondition: Electron
  // creates a preload realm (a blink ShadowRealm) in every SW renderer.
  ses.registerPreloadScript({
    type: "service-worker",
    filePath: path.join(__dirname, "sw-preload.js"),
  });

  ses.serviceWorkers.on("running-status-changed", (d) => {
    console.log(`SW ${d.versionId}: ${d.runningStatus}`);
  });
  ses.serviceWorkers.on("console-message", (_e, d) => {
    console.log(`[sw] ${d?.message ?? ""}`);
  });

  await ses.extensions.loadExtension(path.join(__dirname, "ext"));
});
```

**`sw-preload.js`** — allocation churn + forced GC *inside the preload realm*, so V8 attributes the GC metrics event to the ShadowRealm context:
```js
console.log("preload realm alive, gc =", typeof globalThis.gc);
(async () => {
  for (let n = 0; n < 5000; n++) {
    const junk = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) junk.push(new Array(20000).fill(i));
    globalThis.gc?.();
    if (n % 100 === 0) console.log("cycle", n);
    await new Promise((r) => queueMicrotask(r));
  }
  console.log("survived — not reproduced");
})();
```

**`ext/manifest.json`**
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "sw-gc-crash-repro",
  "version": "1.0",
  "background": { "service_worker": "background.js" }
}
```
(`ext/background.js` can be empty.)

**Observed on Electron 42.5.2 (macOS arm64):** the SW renderer dies almost immediately after `cycle 0`; the worker loops `starting` → dead 3-4 times and then Chromium gives up:

```
SW 3: starting
[sw] preload realm alive, gc = function
[sw] cycle 0
SW 3: starting
[sw] preload realm alive, gc = function
[sw] cycle 0
...
ERROR:extensions/browser/service_worker/service_worker_task_queue.cc DidStartWorkerFail
```

In a real app (our browser, with ~10 MV3 extensions installed) this crash loop plus the app's own SW restart machinery saturates the browser-process main thread with process spawning and freezes the app for minutes (macOS hang reports show all samples in `StartWorker → AllocateWorkerProcess`, kernel leaves in `getentropy`).

## Root cause (exact line)

Symbolicated production crash: `blink::V8MetricsRecorder::GetUkmRecorderAndSourceId` at `v8_metrics.cc:662` — i.e. the `context->UkmRecorder()` virtual call:

1. V8 emits a GC metrics event attributed to the context in which the collection was triggered — here the **service-worker preload realm**.
2. Electron's preload realm is built on blink's ShadowRealm machinery (`shell/renderer/preload_realm_context.cc` creates a `ShadowRealmGlobalScope` + real `ScriptState`), so `ExecutionContext::From()` resolves it fine.
3. But `ShadowRealmGlobalScope`'s UKM accessors are `NOTREACHED()` (Chromium 148, `third_party/blink/renderer/core/shadow_realm/shadow_realm_global_scope.cc:123`):

```cc
ukm::UkmRecorder* ShadowRealmGlobalScope::UkmRecorder() {
  NOTREACHED();
}

ukm::SourceId ShadowRealmGlobalScope::UkmSourceID() const {
  NOTREACHED();
}
```

Upstream blink assumed no GC metrics event would ever be attributed to a ShadowRealm; Electron's SW preload realms make that assumption false whenever a full GC happens while the realm context is active.

## Proposed fix (4 lines, chromium patch)

The caller already null-checks the recorder (`GetUkmRecorderAndSourceId`, v8_metrics.cc:661-663), so reporting "no UKM" is safe and correct — ShadowRealms have no UKM source by design:

```cc
ukm::UkmRecorder* ShadowRealmGlobalScope::UkmRecorder() {
  return nullptr;
}

ukm::SourceId ShadowRealmGlobalScope::UkmSourceID() const {
  return ukm::kInvalidSourceId;
}
```

We are currently building Electron v42.5.2 with this patch to confirm the repro above stops reproducing, and can send it as `patches/chromium/` PR if that helps.

Fix submitted as #52386.
